window.QUARTERS.push({"id":"fall-2026","label":"Fall Quarter 2026","start":"2026-09-08","end":"2026-11-20","program":"M.A. Media and Globalization, Public Diplomacy certificate","courses":[{"code":"MFJS 4160","slug":"mfjs4160","title":"Media Theories","instructor":"Dr. Rachael Liberman","text":"Baran & Davis, Mass Communication Theory (8th ed.)","description":"Mass communication theory in the order it was argued: mass society, limited effects, the critical cultural turn, and meaning making, tested against platforms, data, and identity.","milestones":[["2026-10-05","Theory profile essay"],["2026-10-28","Application essay"],["2026-11-16","Peer review"],["2026-11-18","Theoretical framework"]]},{"code":"MFJS 4650","slug":"mfjs4650","title":"Global Media and Communication","instructor":"Dr. Erika Polson","text":"Regional focus: Eastern Europe","description":"How media moves across borders: imperial networks and news agencies, the cultural imperialism debates, globalization and deterritorialization, streaming, and platform power.","milestones":[["2026-09-28","Western Europe case study"],["2026-10-14","Midterm"],["2026-10-19","Debate"],["2026-11-04","Eastern Europe case study"],["2026-11-19","Position paper"]]}],"deepDives":[{"title":"From mass society to libertarian theory","status":"In progress","course":"MFJS 4160","due":"2026-10-05","summary":"A theory profile tracing mass society thinking into libertarian press theory and the self-righting principle, following both through each paradigm shift and into current generational research for and against them.","sources":["Milton (1644)","Lippmann (1922)","Keane (1991)","Napoli (1999)","Adams-Bloom & Cleary (2009)"]},{"title":"How algorithms classify people, 1841 to 2026","status":"Synthesis complete","course":"Independent research","due":null,"summary":"Four eras of sorting people: actuarial and geodemographic clusters, collaborative filtering, footprint inference, and embeddings with generative recommenders. Classification moved from declared, to inferred, to implicit.","sources":["Cheney-Lippold (2011)","Kosinski et al. (2013)","Fourcade & Healy (2017)"]},{"title":"Culture as window dressing: Netflix and locality","status":"Presented Sep 28","course":"MFJS 4650","due":null,"summary":"Western Europe case study on Idiz et al. (2025), read through Tomlinson on deterritorialization and Appadurai's five scapes, with the Dutch streaming investment obligation as the policy sequel.","sources":["Idiz et al. (2025)","Tomlinson (1999)","Appadurai (1990)","Hamelink (1983)"]},{"title":"Eastern Europe: public diplomacy and soft power","status":"Starting","course":"MFJS 4650","due":"2026-11-04","summary":"Cold war propaganda and free flow, post-1989 media privatization, media capture, and platform governance under the Digital Services Act, building toward the regional case study and the final position paper.","sources":["Thussu (2019)","Entman (1993)","Helberg (2021)"]},{"title":"Thesis concept: classifying relationships","status":"In development","course":"Master's thesis","due":null,"summary":"Building a research database of professional relationships while theorizing what classification does to the people it sorts. The guard rules on automated profiling are where the tool and the critique meet.","sources":["Cheney-Lippold (2011)","Fourcade & Healy (2017)","Couldry & Mejias (2019)"]}],"readings":[{"id":"henderson-2020","course":"mfjs4160","date":"2026-09-13","status":"read","kind":"article","short":"Henderson (2020)","title":"Media studies futures: Whiteness, indigeneity, multi-modality, and a politics of possibility","apa":"Henderson, L. (2020). Media studies futures: Whiteness, indigeneity, multi-modality, and a politics of possibility. *Television & New Media, 21*(6), 581-589. https://doi.org/10.1177/1527476420921515","access":"paywalled","read_url":null,"doi_url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/1527476420921515","isbn10":null,"summary":"Henderson, writing as a media studies dean, reflects on where the field should go next. Using Hannah Gadsby's stand-up special Nanette as a touchstone, she argues that the discipline needs to divest from its default whiteness, take Indigenous knowledge and multi-modal scholarship seriously, and cultivate a politics of possibility rather than only critique.","analysis":"The essay is a programmatic intervention rather than an empirical study, and its evidence is institutional experience, the #CommunicationSoWhite critique of citation and hiring patterns, and a close reading of a comedic performance. Its main contribution is to treat the field's future as a question of who produces knowledge and in what forms, not only what topics it studies. By pairing divestment from whiteness with investment in Indigenous and multi-modal work, it links representation inside the academy to the representational critiques media studies aims at industry. A limit is that the prescription stays at the level of disposition and institutional will, with few concrete mechanisms for redistributing resources or authority, and it is written largely from a Canadian and Anglo-American vantage. It sets up the course's recurring lens on centering whiteness and the West, which returns in Hall's white eye, Steele, Brock, and Couldry and Mejias.","themes":["future of the field","whiteness","indigeneity","multi-modality","disciplinary critique"],"confidence":"confirmed","note":"Her notes list this as Communication and Critical/Cultural Studies 17(1); Crossref shows the article titled Media Studies Futures is in Television & New Media 21(6), 2020. Confirm against the syllabus PDF."},{"id":"hearn-2013","course":"mfjs4160","date":"2026-09-13","status":"read","kind":"article","short":"Hearn (2013)","title":"Situation critical","apa":"Hearn, A. (2013). Situation critical. *Communication and Critical/Cultural Studies, 10*(2-3), 273-279. https://doi.org/10.1080/14791420.2013.814414","access":"paywalled","read_url":null,"doi_url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/14791420.2013.814414","isbn10":null,"summary":"Part of a keywords forum in which scholars reflected on the three terms in the journal's title, Hearn asks what being critical can still mean when critique itself has become a marketable posture. She argues for critique grounded in a clear reading of the present conjuncture of capitalism, labor, and self-branding, rather than critique as a signature style.","analysis":"Hearn works through immanent critique: she tests the field's claims to criticality against the political economic conditions in which academics and media workers now labor. Her contribution is to connect the academic word critical to her broader research on promotional culture and the branded self, showing that the university and the platform economy share a logic of self-promotion. The short forum format makes the piece suggestive rather than systematic, and its examples come mostly from North American and British contexts. It pairs with Henderson as a first-week framing of what the course means by critical, and it previews the chapter 6 split between political economy and cultural studies, as well as Banet-Weiser on branded authenticity and Smythe on audience labor.","themes":["critique","immanent critique","promotional culture","political economy","keywords"],"confidence":"confirmed","note":""},{"id":"baran-davis-ch1","course":"mfjs4160","date":"2026-09-13","status":"read","kind":"textbook-chapter","short":"Baran & Davis ch. 1","title":"Understanding and evaluating mass communication theory","apa":"Baran, S. J., & Davis, D. K. (2021). Understanding and evaluating mass communication theory. In *Mass communication theory: Foundations, ferment, and future* (8th ed.). Oxford University Press.","access":"book","read_url":null,"doi_url":null,"isbn10":"0190942770","summary":"The opening chapter defines theory and explains why mass communication has several kinds of it rather than one. It introduces the ontological, epistemological, and axiological commitments that separate postpositivist, hermeneutic, critical, and normative theory, and it previews the historical trends the book will trace.","analysis":"The chapter's central move is to treat theory as a set of competing ways of knowing, each with its own standards of evidence and its own stance on values. Its framework for evaluating theory gives students a vocabulary they will reuse all term when weighing strengths and weaknesses in the Instant Access boxes. A strength is its insistence that no single paradigm owns the field, which legitimizes the critical and cultural work assigned later. A limit is that the paradigm story is told from a largely United States disciplinary history, so non-Western theory appears mostly as context rather than as a source of theory. Read with Henderson and Hearn, it raises the question of whether the textbook's taxonomy itself reproduces the whiteness those essays critique.","themes":["theory","epistemology","axiology","paradigms","scientific method"],"confidence":"confirmed","note":""},{"id":"baran-davis-ch2","course":"mfjs4160","date":"2026-09-16","status":"read","kind":"textbook-chapter","short":"Baran & Davis ch. 2","title":"Establishing the terms of the debate over media: The first trend in mass communication theory: Mass society and propaganda theories","apa":"Baran, S. J., & Davis, D. K. (2021). Establishing the terms of the debate over media: The first trend in mass communication theory: Mass society and propaganda theories. In *Mass communication theory: Foundations, ferment, and future* (8th ed.). Oxford University Press.","access":"book","read_url":null,"doi_url":null,"isbn10":"0190942770","summary":"The chapter traces mass society theory from late nineteenth century elite anxieties about industrialization and the penny press, and then turns to interwar propaganda theory. It closes by showing how both traditions return in current debates about populism, disinformation, and platform manipulation.","analysis":"Baran and Davis reconstruct mass society theory as six assumptions, from powerful direct media effects to the debasement of high culture, and ground it in Tonnies and Durkheim. The propaganda section links behaviorism and Freudian psychology to Lasswell's master symbols, Lippmann's pseudo-environment, and Dewey's democratic reply. The chapter's contribution is to show that the first trend set the terms of later debate, even where later scholars rejected its conclusions. A limit, noted by historians such as Sproule and Pooley, is that the tidy magic bullet story is partly a straw man built by the later effects school, and the chapter only partly corrects that myth. It pairs directly with Lippmann and sets up the normative theories in chapter 3.","themes":["mass society","propaganda","direct effects","Lippmann","Dewey"],"confidence":"confirmed","note":""},{"id":"lippmann-1922","course":"mfjs4160","date":"2026-09-16","status":"read","kind":"chapter","short":"Lippmann (1922)","title":"The world outside and the pictures in our heads","apa":"Lippmann, W. (1922). The world outside and the pictures in our heads. In *Public opinion* (pp. 3-32). Harcourt, Brace.","access":"free","read_url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6456/pg6456-images.html","doi_url":null,"isbn10":"0684833271","summary":"The first chapter of Public Opinion opens with islanders who lived as friends for weeks after their nations went to war because the news had not arrived. Lippmann argues that everyone acts on a mental picture of the world that sits between them and reality, and that this pseudo-environment, not the world itself, drives behavior.","analysis":"Lippmann builds his case through vivid historical anecdotes rather than systematic data: wartime hero worship, medieval maps, and staged military photographs. His key contribution is the pseudo-environment, a concept that makes mediated representation the central problem of democratic life and anticipates agenda setting and framing. He treats fictions as necessary simplifications, not lies, and locates the difficulty in the scale and complexity of modern society rather than in popular stupidity. The strongest critique is that his later technocratic remedy, an expert bureau filtering information, sidelines public deliberation, which is exactly what Dewey attacked. Read against chapter 2, the primary text is more careful than the textbook's bewildered herd summary, and it anticipates algorithmic feeds as machine-built pictures in our heads.","themes":["pseudo-environment","public opinion","stereotypes","democracy","representation"],"confidence":"confirmed","note":"Public domain. Page range is for the Harcourt, Brace first edition chapter 1; ISBN is the Free Press paperback. Her notes also cite the New Republic serialization (1922, vol. 30, pp. 10-14)."},{"id":"baran-davis-ch3","course":"mfjs4160","date":"2026-09-21","status":"read","kind":"textbook-chapter","short":"Baran & Davis ch. 3","title":"Normative theories of mass communication","apa":"Baran, S. J., & Davis, D. K. (2021). Normative theories of mass communication. In *Mass communication theory: Foundations, ferment, and future* (8th ed.). Oxford University Press.","access":"book","read_url":null,"doi_url":null,"isbn10":"0190942770","summary":"The chapter surveys prescriptive theories about how media ought to operate, from authoritarian and libertarian models through social responsibility theory and the Hutchins Commission. It extends the discussion to the role of journalism ethics, objectivity, and the regulation of online platforms.","analysis":"Normative theory is judged here by its ideals rather than by empirical prediction, and the chapter traces how libertarian faith in a self-righting marketplace of ideas gave way to social responsibility after the 1947 Hutchins report. Its contribution is to show that media policy debates, from the Fairness Doctrine to Section 230, are arguments among these normative models. The chapter usefully flags the benevolent technocracy at the heart of social responsibility, where experts decide what the public needs. A limit is its heavy focus on the United States First Amendment tradition, with other national and non-Western press systems treated mainly as foils. It pairs with Adams-Bloom and Cleary, who try to reconcile profit and public service, and with Couldry and Mejias later on platform governance.","themes":["normative theory","libertarian theory","social responsibility","Hutchins Commission","press freedom"],"confidence":"confirmed","note":""},{"id":"adams-bloom-cleary-2009","course":"mfjs4160","date":"2026-09-21","status":"read","kind":"article","short":"Adams-Bloom & Cleary (2009)","title":"Staking a claim for social responsibility: An argument for the dual responsibility model","apa":"Adams-Bloom, T., & Cleary, J. (2009). Staking a claim for social responsibility: An argument for the dual responsibility model. *International Journal on Media Management, 11*(1), 1-8. https://doi.org/10.1080/14241270802518356","access":"paywalled","read_url":null,"doi_url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/14241270802518356","isbn10":null,"summary":"The authors argue that news organizations should be understood as having two obligations at once, to shareholders and to the public. They propose a dual responsibility model that borrows from stakeholder theory to show how media firms can remain profitable while meeting social responsibility norms.","analysis":"This is a conceptual essay that updates social responsibility theory for an era of publicly traded, consolidated media companies. Its contribution is to move the normative debate out of journalism ethics and into management theory, treating public service as a stakeholder claim a firm must balance. The argument is brief and largely untested, and it assumes that market pressure and civic duty can be reconciled through managerial will. Critics in the political economy tradition would reply that the ownership structures described by Smythe and chapter 6 make that balance structurally unstable. The model also anticipates later debates over whether profit-driven platforms can be asked to serve the public, taken up by Couldry and Mejias and by Roberts.","themes":["social responsibility","stakeholder theory","media management","profit vs public interest"],"confidence":"confirmed","note":""},{"id":"baran-davis-ch4","course":"mfjs4160","date":"2026-09-23","status":"read","kind":"textbook-chapter","short":"Baran & Davis ch. 4","title":"The emergence of the media-effects trend in mass communication theory","apa":"Baran, S. J., & Davis, D. K. (2021). The emergence of the media-effects trend in mass communication theory. In *Mass communication theory: Foundations, ferment, and future* (8th ed.). Oxford University Press.","access":"book","read_url":null,"doi_url":null,"isbn10":"0190942770","summary":"This chapter follows the turn from speculative mass society theory to empirical research in the 1940s, led by Lazarsfeld's survey work and Hovland's persuasion experiments. It explains how findings of limited, conditional effects produced a new paradigm.","analysis":"The chapter's argument is that method changed theory: once researchers used panel surveys and controlled experiments, they found that media usually reinforced existing attitudes rather than converting people. It introduces the People's Choice study, the two-step flow, selective processes, and the Yale persuasion variables. A strength is its clear account of how postpositivist standards became the field's default. A limit, which later critics such as Gitlin pressed, is that the limited effects label served media industries and funders and narrowed attention to short-term individual attitude change. It frames Hovland, Janis, and Kelley directly and sets up chapter 6's critical reaction.","themes":["media effects","limited effects","Lazarsfeld","persuasion research","postpositivism"],"confidence":"confirmed","note":""},{"id":"hovland-janis-kelley-1953","course":"mfjs4160","date":"2026-09-23","status":"read","kind":"book","short":"Hovland, Janis & Kelley (1953)","title":"Communication and persuasion: Psychological studies of opinion change","apa":"Hovland, C. I., Janis, I. L., & Kelley, H. H. (1953). *Communication and persuasion: Psychological studies of opinion change*. Yale University Press.","access":"book","read_url":"https://archive.org/details/communicationper0000unse","doi_url":null,"isbn10":null,"summary":"The Yale Communication Research Program's summary volume reports a series of experiments on what makes persuasive messages work. It examines the credibility of the communicator, fear appeals, message organization, group membership, individual differences in persuasibility, and how long attitude change lasts.","analysis":"The book models the controlled variation approach: isolate one feature of a message or source, manipulate it, and measure attitude change in student or military samples. Its lasting contributions include source credibility research and the sleeper effect, the finding that a low-credibility message can gain influence over time as its source is forgotten. It made persuasion a program of cumulative, testable variables and trained a generation of effects researchers. The major limit is external validity: short laboratory exposures with mostly white, male, American participants say little about long-term cultural influence or audiences outside that frame. Chapter 4 presents it as a founding effects text, and chapter 6 and Hall later show what this variable-based approach could not see.","themes":["persuasion","source credibility","experiments","attitude change","Yale program"],"confidence":"confirmed","note":"read_url is an Internet Archive controlled digital lending copy (free borrow with account). No ISBN on the 1953 edition."},{"id":"baran-davis-ch5","course":"mfjs4160","date":"2026-09-28","status":"read","kind":"textbook-chapter","short":"Baran & Davis ch. 5","title":"The consolidation of the media effects trend","apa":"Baran, S. J., & Davis, D. K. (2021). The consolidation of the media effects trend. In *Mass communication theory: Foundations, ferment, and future* (8th ed.). Oxford University Press.","access":"book","read_url":null,"doi_url":null,"isbn10":"0190942770","summary":"The chapter shows how limited effects findings hardened into a dominant perspective through Klapper's phenomenistic approach, functionalism, and information flow and diffusion research. It explains why the effects trend dominated American communication research through the 1960s.","analysis":"Baran and Davis argue that the effects trend consolidated because it fit the era's institutional funding, its faith in science, and its pluralist view of democracy. Klapper's synthesis, the two-step flow, and diffusion of innovations are presented as its high points, along with functional analysis of what media do for society. The chapter credits the trend with rigor and cumulative findings. It also notes the cost: a focus on short-term individual effects and an assumption that the social order was basically sound. It frames Katz and Lazarsfeld and anticipates the critical cultural backlash in chapter 6.","themes":["limited effects","Klapper","functionalism","diffusion","two-step flow"],"confidence":"confirmed","note":""},{"id":"katz-lazarsfeld-1955","course":"mfjs4160","date":"2026-09-28","status":"read","kind":"book","short":"Katz & Lazarsfeld (1955)","title":"Personal influence: The part played by people in the flow of mass communications","apa":"Katz, E., & Lazarsfeld, P. F. (1955). *Personal influence: The part played by people in the flow of mass communications*. Free Press.","access":"book","read_url":"https://archive.org/details/personalinfluenc0000katz","doi_url":null,"isbn10":"1412805074","summary":"Based on interviews with about 800 women in Decatur, Illinois, the book examines how opinions about marketing, fashion, public affairs, and movies spread through everyday conversation. It develops the two-step flow model, in which opinion leaders attend to media and pass ideas on to others through personal networks.","analysis":"The Decatur study used survey interviews and follow-ups to trace influence episodes, and it found that opinion leaders were spread across social strata rather than concentrated at the top. Its central contribution is to rediscover the primary group, showing that interpersonal relationships mediate media influence. The introduction also helped invent the story that earlier research assumed atomized audiences, a narrative later historians such as Pooley call an origin myth. Critics such as Gitlin note that the study was funded by a publisher interested in reaching consumers, that much opinion change involved no remembered personal contact, and that its focus on women as consumers reflects the gendered assumptions of 1950s market research. It extends chapter 5 and returns in platform research on influencers and networked two-step flow.","themes":["two-step flow","opinion leaders","interpersonal influence","limited effects","survey research"],"confidence":"confirmed","note":"read_url is an Internet Archive controlled digital lending copy of the 1964 printing. ISBN is the current Routledge/Transaction paperback."},{"id":"nguyen-2023","course":"mfjs4160","date":"2026-09-28","status":"read","kind":"article","short":"Nguyen (2023)","title":"The political economy of heteronormativity","apa":"Nguyen, D. H. (2023). The political economy of heteronormativity. *Review of Radical Political Economics, 55*(1), 112-131. https://doi.org/10.1177/04866134211011269","access":"paywalled","read_url":null,"doi_url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/04866134211011269","isbn10":null,"summary":"Nguyen revisits the Fraser and Butler debate over whether queer oppression is economic or cultural, reading it through social reproduction theory. The article argues that heteronormativity helps capitalism reproduce itself and that neoliberalism now divides queer people with capital from those without.","analysis":"The method is theoretical synthesis, drawing on Marxist feminist social reproduction theory to reframe a debate usually staged as recognition versus redistribution. Its contribution is to show that heteronormative family forms perform material work for capital, which means queer liberation requires political economic change, not only visibility. It also critiques mainstream LGBT progress narratives for masking class stratification among queer people. A limit is that the argument stays at the level of theory with little empirical case material, and its account of neoliberalism is largely drawn from North American and European contexts. It links the political economy tradition in chapter 6 and Smythe to later readings on visibility and representation, including Cavalcante, Warner, and Banet-Weiser.","themes":["political economy","heteronormativity","social reproduction","queer theory","neoliberalism"],"confidence":"confirmed","note":"Classmate's article presentation on Sep 28. Online-first 2021, print issue 2023."},{"id":"baran-davis-ch6","course":"mfjs4160","date":"2026-09-30","status":"read","kind":"textbook-chapter","short":"Baran & Davis ch. 6","title":"The emergence of the critical cultural trend in North America","apa":"Baran, S. J., & Davis, D. K. (2021). The emergence of the critical cultural trend in North America. In *Mass communication theory: Foundations, ferment, and future* (8th ed.). Oxford University Press.","access":"book","read_url":null,"doi_url":null,"isbn10":"0190942770","summary":"The chapter explains how critical and cultural theories rose in the 1960s and 1970s as the effects paradigm lost explanatory power. It traces Marxism, the Frankfurt School, British cultural studies, political economy, Carey's ritual view, news production research, critical feminist scholarship, and McLuhan.","analysis":"Baran and Davis frame the trend as a shift from measuring effects to interpreting how media shape culture, identity, and power over the long term. The chapter's key distinctions are micro cultural studies versus macro political economy, structuralist versus culturalist Marxism, and transmission versus ritual models of communication. It credits the trend with asking big questions about control and ownership while noting its lack of scientific verification. A limit is that critical theory is narrated largely as a European import to North America, so scholars of color and anti-colonial thinkers appear late, mostly under intersectionality. It is the theoretical frame for Hall's essay, for Smythe on Oct 5, and for most readings in the second half of the course.","themes":["critical theory","cultural studies","political economy","ritual communication","news production"],"confidence":"confirmed","note":""},{"id":"hall-1981","course":"mfjs4160","date":"2026-09-30","status":"read","kind":"chapter","short":"Hall (1981)","title":"The whites of their eyes: Racist ideologies and the media","apa":"Hall, S. (2021). The whites of their eyes: Racist ideologies and the media. In C. Brunsdon (Ed.), *Writings on media: History of the present* (pp. 177-200). Duke University Press. (Original work published 1981)","access":"book","read_url":null,"doi_url":"https://www.dukeupress.edu/writings-on-media","isbn10":"1478014717","summary":"Hall asks how British media construct the meaning of race so as to reproduce racist ideology, often without anyone intending it, and how the left can intervene. He distinguishes overt racism from inferential racism, traces a colonial grammar of race in television genres, and defends a popular strategy for anti-racist media work.","analysis":"The essay combines a theory of ideology as chains of meaning with close readings of British adventure films, sitcoms, and news coverage such as the 1979 Southall protests. Its central contribution is inferential racism: racist premises built into apparently neutral or liberal representations that are invisible even to those who produce them, so bias does not require intent. Hall rejects both conspiracy models of media and economic determinism, treating media as a plural but unequal site of struggle, which is the culturalist position chapter 6 names. A limit is that the analysis is rooted in 1970s British television and does not systematically examine audiences, so claims about how viewers take up these images remain inferred. It anticipates Brock, Steele, and later work on algorithmic classification, where bias lives in system premises rather than individual intent.","themes":["ideology","inferential racism","representation","cultural studies","race"],"confidence":"confirmed","note":"Verified: chapter 14 of the Duke University Press volume in the Stuart Hall: Selected Writings series (Brunsdon, Ed., 2021). Originally in G. Bridges & R. Brunt (Eds.), Silver linings: Some strategies for the eighties (pp. 28-52), Lawrence and Wishart. It is not in the Essential Essays volumes."},{"id":"smythe-1977","course":"mfjs4160","date":"2026-10-05","status":"upcoming","kind":"article","short":"Smythe (1977)","title":"Communications: Blindspot of Western Marxism","apa":"Smythe, D. W. (1977). Communications: Blindspot of Western Marxism. *Canadian Journal of Political and Social Theory, 1*(3), 1-27.","access":"free","read_url":"https://journals.uvic.ca/index.php/ctheory/article/download/13715/4463","doi_url":"https://journals.uvic.ca/index.php/ctheory/article/view/13715","isbn10":null,"summary":"Smythe argues that Western Marxists misread mass media by treating them mainly as producers of ideology. He proposes that the main commodity of advertiser-supported media is the audience itself, whose attention is sold to advertisers, so audiences perform unpaid work for capital.","analysis":"This is a polemical theoretical essay that reframes media economics through Marx's labor theory of value. Its contribution, the audience commodity thesis, made advertising and audience measurement central to political economy and set off the blindspot debate with Murdock and others. The thesis now reads as prophetic for platforms that monetize user attention and data. Critics argue that watching is not labor in any strict sense, that the model neglects how audiences interpret content, and that it generalizes from North American commercial broadcasting to media everywhere. It is the political economy counterpart to Hall in chapter 6, and it leads directly to Couldry and Mejias on data colonialism and Roberts on platform labor.","themes":["political economy","audience commodity","advertising","Marxism","labor"],"confidence":"likely","note":"Syllabus lists only Smythe on this date with no chapter; chose his best-known political economy essay, which follows chapter 6's political economy section. Free from the University of Victoria journal archive."},{"id":"baran-davis-ch7","course":"mfjs4160","date":"2026-10-07","status":"upcoming","kind":"textbook-chapter","short":"Baran & Davis ch. 7","title":"Theories of media and social learning","apa":"Baran, S. J., & Davis, D. K. (2021). Theories of media and social learning. In *Mass communication theory: Foundations, ferment, and future* (8th ed.). Oxford University Press.","access":"book","read_url":null,"doi_url":null,"isbn10":"0190942770","summary":"The chapter covers theories that explain how people learn behavior from media, with a focus on the long debate over television violence. It moves from early violence research to Bandura's social learning and social cognitive theory, and then to the general aggression model and newer media.","analysis":"The chapter's argument is that social learning theory gave effects research a sturdier model than simple imitation by adding attention, retention, motivation, and self-efficacy. It reviews the Surgeon General reports and experimental and longitudinal evidence on aggression, noting why industry and researchers disagreed over the size of effects. A strength is its demonstration that effects research did not end with limited effects but moved toward conditional, cumulative claims. A limit is that the research base relies heavily on lab studies of American children, and the chapter pays less attention to how race, class, and gender shape which models are shown and imitated. It frames Bandura and connects forward to cultivation in chapter 12.","themes":["social learning","media violence","Bandura","modeling","aggression"],"confidence":"confirmed","note":""},{"id":"bandura-2001","course":"mfjs4160","date":"2026-10-07","status":"upcoming","kind":"article","short":"Bandura (2001)","title":"Social cognitive theory of mass communication","apa":"Bandura, A. (2001). Social cognitive theory of mass communication. *Media Psychology, 3*(3), 265-299. https://doi.org/10.1207/S1532785XMEP0303_03","access":"paywalled","read_url":null,"doi_url":"https://doi.org/10.1207/S1532785XMEP0303_03","isbn10":null,"summary":"Bandura lays out how symbolic modeling in media shapes thought, emotion, and behavior through attention, retention, production, and motivation. He adds a dual path account in which media influence people both directly and through the social networks that adopt what media model, illustrated with entertainment education campaigns.","analysis":"The article is a theoretical synthesis of decades of experimental work, framed by triadic reciprocal causation among person, behavior, and environment. Its contribution is to position audiences as agents with self-regulatory capacities while still explaining powerful cumulative media influence. The entertainment education examples, including serial dramas in Mexico, India, and Tanzania, show how the theory was applied to health and social change. A critique is that those campaigns often exported Western development priorities, and the model treats culture as a set of behaviors to be shaped rather than as meanings audiences negotiate. It connects to chapter 7, to the two-step flow through its social pathway, and contrasts with Hall and Gray's more interpretive audiences.","themes":["social cognitive theory","modeling","self-efficacy","entertainment education","media effects"],"confidence":"likely","note":"Syllabus lists only Bandura; chose his 2001 statement specifically about mass communication, which pairs with chapter 7."},{"id":"baran-davis-ch8","course":"mfjs4160","date":"2026-10-12","status":"upcoming","kind":"textbook-chapter","short":"Baran & Davis ch. 8","title":"Theories of media and human development","apa":"Baran, S. J., & Davis, D. K. (2021). Theories of media and human development. In *Mass communication theory: Foundations, ferment, and future* (8th ed.). Oxford University Press.","access":"book","read_url":null,"doi_url":null,"isbn10":"0190942770","summary":"The chapter examines how media shape development across the life course, especially childhood and adolescence. It covers active theories of children's television viewing, the idea of media as a super peer, body image and sexualization research, and the developmental effects of digital media.","analysis":"Baran and Davis organize the chapter around the claim that media are a primary agent of socialization, competing with family, school, and peers. The chapter weighs active and reactive models of young audiences and reviews the evidence on media and body image, gender roles, and sexual attitudes. It is strong in showing how developmental psychology fed communication theory. A limit is that much of the reviewed research assumes a white, middle-class Western child as the norm and measures harm more readily than agency or pleasure. It frames Fredrickson et al. and objectification theory and anticipates the postfeminism debates on Nov 4.","themes":["human development","socialization","children and media","body image","adolescence"],"confidence":"confirmed","note":""},{"id":"fredrickson-et-al-1998","course":"mfjs4160","date":"2026-10-12","status":"upcoming","kind":"article","short":"Fredrickson et al. (1998)","title":"That swimsuit becomes you: Sex differences in self-objectification, restrained eating, and math performance","apa":"Fredrickson, B. L., Roberts, T.-A., Noll, S. M., Quinn, D. M., & Twenge, J. M. (1998). That swimsuit becomes you: Sex differences in self-objectification, restrained eating, and math performance. *Journal of Personality and Social Psychology, 75*(1), 269-284. https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-3514.75.1.269","access":"paywalled","read_url":null,"doi_url":"https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-3514.75.1.269","isbn10":null,"summary":"In two experiments, participants tried on either a swimsuit or a sweater alone in a dressing room and then completed tasks. Women in swimsuits reported more body shame, ate less, and did worse on a math test, while men showed little effect, supporting the idea that a self-objectifying state costs women emotionally and cognitively.","analysis":"The study operationalizes objectification theory, proposed by Fredrickson and Roberts in 1997, which holds that cultures that sexually objectify women teach them to monitor their bodies from an observer's view. Its contribution is experimental evidence that a brief state of self-objectification has measurable consequences for eating and cognitive performance. The design is clever but relies on white American undergraduates in a contrived setting, so its generalization across race, class, sexuality, and culture is limited, and it does not directly test media exposure. Its concept of an internalized observer echoes Mulvey's male gaze and Hall's white eye from chapter 6. It anchors chapter 8's body image section and sets up Banet-Weiser and Dosekun on how postfeminist culture reworks self-surveillance as empowerment.","themes":["objectification theory","self-objectification","body image","gender","experiments"],"confidence":"uncertain","note":"Syllabus lists Fredrickson et al. The et al. points to the five-author 1998 experiment rather than Fredrickson and Roberts (1997), Objectification theory, Psychology of Women Quarterly, 21(2), 173-206, which is the theory statement. Check the syllabus PDF."},{"id":"baran-davis-ch9","course":"mfjs4160","date":"2026-10-14","status":"upcoming","kind":"textbook-chapter","short":"Baran & Davis ch. 9","title":"Audience theories: Uses and reception","apa":"Baran, S. J., & Davis, D. K. (2021). Audience theories: Uses and reception. In *Mass communication theory: Foundations, ferment, and future* (8th ed.). Oxford University Press.","access":"book","read_url":null,"doi_url":null,"isbn10":"0190942770","summary":"The chapter presents theories that treat audiences as active: uses and gratifications, reception studies, and Hall's encoding and decoding model. It also covers fandom, entertainment theory, and how these approaches apply to interactive and social media.","analysis":"The chapter's argument is that shifting the question from what media do to people to what people do with media transformed the field. Uses and gratifications brought a functional, survey-based account of needs, while reception studies, following Hall and Morley, brought an interpretive account of dominant, negotiated, and oppositional readings. A strength is its bridge between postpositivist and cultural traditions. A limit is that the celebration of active audiences can obscure the structural power political economists stress, a tension the chapter itself raises. It frames Gray's work on fans and anti-fans and links back to Hall and forward to Cavalcante's audience study.","themes":["audience theory","uses and gratifications","reception studies","encoding/decoding","fandom"],"confidence":"confirmed","note":""},{"id":"gray-2003","course":"mfjs4160","date":"2026-10-14","status":"upcoming","kind":"article","short":"Gray (2003)","title":"New audiences, new textualities: Anti-fans and non-fans","apa":"Gray, J. (2003). New audiences, new textualities: Anti-fans and non-fans. *International Journal of Cultural Studies, 6*(1), 64-81. https://doi.org/10.1177/1367877903006001004","access":"paywalled","read_url":null,"doi_url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/1367877903006001004","isbn10":null,"summary":"Gray argues that audience research has focused too narrowly on fans and has ignored anti-fans, who dislike a text intensely, and non-fans, who watch casually. He shows that each group relates to texts at a different distance and therefore encounters a different text.","analysis":"The article is a conceptual intervention in fan and reception studies, supported by examples from online discussion and television viewing. Its main contribution is to show that the text is not a stable object: fans, anti-fans, and non-fans build different versions of it from paratexts, hype, and partial viewing. This extends Hall's encoding and decoding and chapter 9's active audience beyond enthusiasts. A limit is that the evidence is illustrative rather than systematic, and the examples come mainly from Anglo-American commercial television. It connects to Cavalcante's transgender audiences, Warner's publics formed through attention, and Brock's Black Twitter, where dislike and critique are also forms of engagement.","themes":["audiences","anti-fans","fandom","textuality","reception"],"confidence":"uncertain","note":"Syllabus lists only Gray alongside chapter 9 (audience theories). Chose Jonathan Gray's anti-fans article as the best audience-studies fit. Alternatives: Herman Gray, Watching race (1995), or Jonathan Gray, Show sold separately (2010). No class meets Oct 14, but the reading is assigned."},{"id":"warner-2002","course":"mfjs4160","date":"2026-10-19","status":"upcoming","kind":"article","short":"Warner (2002)","title":"Publics and counterpublics","apa":"Warner, M. (2002). Publics and counterpublics. *Public Culture, 14*(1), 49-90. https://doi.org/10.1215/08992363-14-1-49","access":"paywalled","read_url":null,"doi_url":"https://doi.org/10.1215/08992363-14-1-49","isbn10":null,"summary":"Warner theorizes a public as a relation among strangers created by the circulation of texts, rather than a preexisting group. Counterpublics are publics that know themselves to be subordinate and that circulate discourse whose style and address break with dominant norms, with queer publics as a central example.","analysis":"The essay works through a set of propositions about publics, drawing on Habermas while criticizing his rational-critical ideal. Its contribution is to make publics a product of attention and circulation, so that simply paying attention can constitute membership. By defining counterpublics through their manner of address, not just their content, Warner explains why queer and other marginalized discourse is often dismissed as not properly public. A limit is its reliance on print-era circulation and United States queer culture; later scholars ask how race and platform algorithms change who can form and sustain a public. It pairs with Cavalcante, and it is the backbone for Steele's Black digital publics and Brock's Black Twitter.","themes":["publics","counterpublics","public sphere","queer theory","circulation"],"confidence":"likely","note":"Syllabus lists only Warner; chose the Public Culture article, which is also the lead chapter of the 2002 book of the same name."},{"id":"cavalcante-2017","course":"mfjs4160","date":"2026-10-19","status":"upcoming","kind":"article","short":"Cavalcante (2017)","title":"Breaking into transgender life: Transgender audiences' experiences with \"first of its kind\" visibility in popular media","apa":"Cavalcante, A. (2017). Breaking into transgender life: Transgender audiences' experiences with \"first of its kind\" visibility in popular media. *Communication, Culture & Critique, 10*(3), 538-555. https://doi.org/10.1111/cccr.12165","access":"paywalled","read_url":null,"doi_url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/cccr.12165","isbn10":null,"summary":"Drawing on interviews with transgender people, Cavalcante examines how breakout media texts, the first mainstream portrayals of trans lives, figured in audiences' early understanding of themselves. He shows that such texts could open possibilities for identity even when the portrayals were limited or stigmatizing.","analysis":"The study uses qualitative audience interviews, bringing reception studies to a group rarely centered in it. Its contribution is the concept of breakout texts, which captures how first-of-its-kind visibility matters for recognition regardless of a portrayal's quality. It complicates simple positive versus negative image critique by showing that audiences extract resources from flawed representations. A limit is that the sample and texts are mostly American, and the visibility it studies is largely white and mainstream, which leaves open how trans audiences of color read the same texts. It extends Gray and chapter 9 on audiences, pairs with Warner on counterpublics, and connects to Nguyen's warning that visibility can mask economic inequality.","themes":["transgender audiences","visibility","reception","identity","representation"],"confidence":"uncertain","note":"Syllabus lists only Cavalcante. Chose his audience-focused article; strong alternatives are I did it all online (2016), Critical Studies in Media Communication, 33(1), 109-122, which pairs with Warner on online publics, or the book Struggling for ordinary (NYU Press, 2018)."},{"id":"baran-davis-ch11","course":"mfjs4160","date":"2026-10-21","status":"upcoming","kind":"textbook-chapter","short":"Baran & Davis ch. 11","title":"Effect of media on knowledge, information, and perception of social issues","apa":"Baran, S. J., & Davis, D. K. (2021). Effect of media on knowledge, information, and perception of social issues. In *Mass communication theory: Foundations, ferment, and future* (8th ed.). Oxford University Press.","access":"book","read_url":null,"doi_url":null,"isbn10":"0190942770","summary":"The chapter covers theories of how media shape what people know and think about public issues. It reviews the knowledge gap hypothesis, agenda setting and priming, framing, the spiral of silence, and research on news, misinformation, and polarization.","analysis":"Baran and Davis argue that these theories revived powerful effects claims by shifting from attitude change to cognition and salience. Agenda setting and framing are presented as the most productive, with evidence that media influence what issues seem important and how problems are defined. The knowledge gap and spiral of silence show how media effects can deepen inequality and silence minority views. A limit is that most evidence comes from United States news environments and treats publics as national, which fits poorly with networked and diasporic publics. It pairs with Steele on how Black digital publics produce their own knowledge and frames outside the mainstream agenda.","themes":["agenda setting","framing","knowledge gap","spiral of silence","news effects"],"confidence":"confirmed","note":""},{"id":"steele-2021","course":"mfjs4160","date":"2026-10-21","status":"upcoming","kind":"book","short":"Steele (2021)","title":"Digital Black feminism","apa":"Steele, C. K. (2021). *Digital Black feminism*. New York University Press. https://doi.org/10.18574/nyu/9781479808373.001.0001","access":"book","read_url":null,"doi_url":"https://doi.org/10.18574/nyu/9781479808373.001.0001","isbn10":"1479808385","summary":"Steele traces how Black women have used technology, from earlier communication forms to blogs and social media, to build community, share knowledge, and critique power. She argues that Black feminist thought has long shaped digital culture and that Black women's online practices are a form of technical and intellectual labor.","analysis":"The book combines historical analysis with close reading of Black women's blogs and social media, and it grounds digital culture in the Black feminist tradition rather than in Silicon Valley histories. Its contribution is to reposition Black women as theorists and innovators of technology, not only as users or targets of harm. It also documents the costs of that labor, including harassment and uncredited appropriation. A limit is that its focus on the United States leaves transnational Black feminist digital practice for others, and the platform economy's ownership structures get less attention than community practices. It connects to Warner's counterpublics, Brock's Black Twitter, Hall's critique of representation, and Roberts on hidden digital labor.","themes":["digital Black feminism","Black women","counterpublics","technology","digital labor"],"confidence":"uncertain","note":"Syllabus lists only Steele; likely a chapter of Digital Black Feminism (2021). A strong alternative is Steele (2018), Black bloggers and their varied publics, Television & New Media, 19(2), 112-127, https://doi.org/10.1177/1527476417709535, which follows Warner directly. Check which chapter or article is assigned."},{"id":"baran-davis-ch12","course":"mfjs4160","date":"2026-10-26","status":"upcoming","kind":"textbook-chapter","short":"Baran & Davis ch. 12","title":"Effect of media on community and everyday culture","apa":"Baran, S. J., & Davis, D. K. (2021). Effect of media on community and everyday culture. In *Mass communication theory: Foundations, ferment, and future* (8th ed.). Oxford University Press.","access":"book","read_url":null,"doi_url":null,"isbn10":"0190942770","summary":"The chapter examines macro theories about how media shape shared culture and community life. It centers on cultivation analysis, media literacy, and debates about social capital and community in a networked, platform-based environment.","analysis":"The chapter's argument is that some of the most important media effects are slow, cumulative, and shared, visible only at the level of culture. Cultivation is presented as the leading example, with its claims about mainstreaming and resonance, and the chapter weighs the method disputes about measuring viewing and perceptions. It also covers media literacy as a practical response to cultural influence. A limit is that cultivation assumes a relatively centralized television system, which strains in fragmented, algorithmic media markets. It frames Gerbner and Gross and reconnects to mass society concerns about isolation and community from chapter 2.","themes":["cultivation","community","social capital","media literacy","everyday culture"],"confidence":"confirmed","note":""},{"id":"gerbner-gross-1976","course":"mfjs4160","date":"2026-10-26","status":"upcoming","kind":"article","short":"Gerbner & Gross (1976)","title":"Living with television: The violence profile","apa":"Gerbner, G., & Gross, L. (1976). Living with television: The violence profile. *Journal of Communication, 26*(2), 172-199. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1460-2466.1976.tb01397.x","access":"paywalled","read_url":null,"doi_url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1460-2466.1976.tb01397.x","isbn10":null,"summary":"Gerbner and Gross introduce cultivation analysis, arguing that television functions as a common storyteller that cultivates shared assumptions about social reality. Using content analysis of prime-time programs and surveys, they report that heavy viewers are more likely than light viewers to overestimate violence and to see the world as dangerous.","analysis":"The Cultural Indicators project combined message system analysis, which counted violence and victimization on television, with cultivation surveys comparing heavy and light viewers. Its contribution was to redefine effects as long-term, shared, and cultural, and to link television violence to social control through fear and the acceptance of authority. The content data also showed that women, older people, and people of color were more often depicted as victims, tying representation to power. Critics such as Hirsch and Newcomb challenged the small correlations, the treatment of viewing as undifferentiated, and inadequate controls for third variables. It extends chapter 12, contrasts with Bandura's individual learning model, and anticipates questions about algorithmic feeds as the new common storyteller.","themes":["cultivation","television violence","mean world","content analysis","cultural indicators"],"confidence":"likely","note":"Syllabus lists only Gerbner; chose the foundational 1976 cultivation article that chapter 12 builds on."},{"id":"baran-davis-ch13","course":"mfjs4160","date":"2026-10-28","status":"upcoming","kind":"textbook-chapter","short":"Baran & Davis ch. 13","title":"Media and culture theories: Meaning making in the social world","apa":"Baran, S. J., & Davis, D. K. (2021). Media and culture theories: Meaning making in the social world. In *Mass communication theory: Foundations, ferment, and future* (8th ed.). Oxford University Press.","access":"book","read_url":null,"doi_url":null,"isbn10":"0190942770","summary":"The chapter presents theories that treat communication as the process through which people construct social reality. It covers symbolic interactionism, social construction of reality, framing as a cultural process, and media ritual and collective memory.","analysis":"Baran and Davis argue that these perspectives shift attention from transmission of information to the shared meanings that make everyday life intelligible. Symbolic interaction and social constructionism explain how identities and institutions are sustained through talk and media, and framing theory is recast as cultural meaning making rather than only a cognitive effect. A strength is the chapter's bridge from Carey's ritual view to contemporary online interaction. A limit is that meaning making is often described in universal terms, with less attention to how race, gender, and power decide whose meanings stick. It pairs with Brock's analysis of how Black Twitter creates and contests meaning.","themes":["social construction","symbolic interactionism","framing","meaning making","ritual"],"confidence":"confirmed","note":""},{"id":"brock-2012","course":"mfjs4160","date":"2026-10-28","status":"upcoming","kind":"article","short":"Brock (2012)","title":"From the blackhand side: Twitter as a cultural conversation","apa":"Brock, A. (2012). From the blackhand side: Twitter as a cultural conversation. *Journal of Broadcasting & Electronic Media, 56*(4), 529-549. https://doi.org/10.1080/08838151.2012.732147","access":"paywalled","read_url":null,"doi_url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/08838151.2012.732147","isbn10":null,"summary":"Brock examines why Black Americans became highly visible on Twitter and how mainstream commentators interpreted that visibility. He argues that Twitter's design fit Black oral and signifying traditions, and that press coverage often read Black Twitter through stereotype rather than as cultural conversation.","analysis":"Brock develops critical technocultural discourse analysis, which reads the platform's interface and affordances alongside users' practices and public discourse about them. His contribution is to treat Black Twitter as cultural production shaped by both technology and Black discursive traditions, not as a demographic curiosity. The article also shows mainstream media reproducing the inferential racism Hall described, framing Black participation as spectacle or deficit. A limit is its reliance on public discourse and platform features from one early moment, before algorithmic timelines and the platform's later ownership changes. It connects to chapter 13 on meaning making, Warner's counterpublics, Steele's digital Black feminism, and Hall.","themes":["Black Twitter","technoculture","critical technocultural discourse analysis","race","signifying"],"confidence":"likely","note":"Syllabus lists only Brock; chose the 2012 Black Twitter article over Distributed Blackness (2020) because a journal article fits a single class day. The book is the alternative if a chapter is assigned."},{"id":"baran-davis-ch14","course":"mfjs4160","date":"2026-11-02","status":"upcoming","kind":"textbook-chapter","short":"Baran & Davis ch. 14","title":"Media and culture theories: Commodification of culture and mediatization","apa":"Baran, S. J., & Davis, D. K. (2021). Media and culture theories: Commodification of culture and mediatization. In *Mass communication theory: Foundations, ferment, and future* (8th ed.). Oxford University Press.","access":"book","read_url":null,"doi_url":null,"isbn10":"0190942770","summary":"The final chapter covers theories about how culture becomes a commodity and how media logics reshape other institutions. It moves from the culture industries and advertising critique to mediatization theory, platform capitalism, and surveillance.","analysis":"The chapter argues that commodification and mediatization together describe a world where culture is produced for markets and where institutions from politics to religion adapt to media logics. It revisits the Frankfurt School and political economy while adding newer accounts of branding, platforms, and data. A strength is how it closes the book by reconnecting early critical theory to digital capitalism. A limit is that mediatization theory, developed mostly in Northern Europe, can overstate media's autonomous logic and flatten differences across regions and communities. It frames Polson's work on geo-social media and place, and prepares for Banet-Weiser, Couldry and Mejias, and Roberts.","themes":["commodification","mediatization","culture industries","platform capitalism","branding"],"confidence":"confirmed","note":""},{"id":"polson-2015","course":"mfjs4160","date":"2026-11-02","status":"upcoming","kind":"article","short":"Polson (2015)","title":"A gateway to the global city: Mobile place-making practices by expats","apa":"Polson, E. (2015). A gateway to the global city: Mobile place-making practices by expats. *New Media & Society, 17*(4), 629-645.","access":"paywalled","read_url":null,"doi_url":null,"isbn10":null,"summary":"Through ethnographic work with expatriate professionals in Paris, Polson studies how platforms such as meetup groups help mobile workers build a sense of belonging. She argues that participants use digital media to make an international community that can be carried from one city to the next.","analysis":"The study uses participant observation and interviews to show how location-based digital organizing turns global cities into navigable places for a mobile professional class. Its contribution is the idea of mobile place-making, which links digital media to a new global middle class, a theme developed in her book Privileged mobilities. Polson recognizes real benefits, especially for women moving alone, while warning that platforms make placelessness a norm of professional labor. A limit is that the focus on relatively privileged, largely Western expats leaves out migrants whose mobility is constrained, although that contrast is part of the argument. It connects to chapter 14 on commodified community, to Couldry and Mejias on platforms extracting social life, and to research on who gets to be mobile in a global media economy.","themes":["digital place-making","mobility","expats","global middle class","geo-social media"],"confidence":"uncertain","note":"Syllabus lists only Polson. Chose her New Media & Society article (online first 2013, print 2015) as the best article-length fit; alternatives are a chapter of Privileged mobilities (Peter Lang, 2016) or Halegoua and Polson (2021), Exploring digital placemaking, Convergence, 27(2). DOI not verified, so doi_url is null."},{"id":"banet-weiser-2018","course":"mfjs4160","date":"2026-11-04","status":"upcoming","kind":"book","short":"Banet-Weiser (2018)","title":"Empowered: Popular feminism and popular misogyny","apa":"Banet-Weiser, S. (2018). *Empowered: Popular feminism and popular misogyny*. Duke University Press.","access":"book","read_url":null,"doi_url":null,"isbn10":"1478002913","summary":"Banet-Weiser analyzes the rise of popular feminism, visible in advertising, celebrity, and social media, alongside a networked popular misogyny that reacts to it. She argues that feminism now circulates in an economy of visibility where being seen is treated as the goal, and that misogyny grows in the same space.","analysis":"The book reads corporate campaigns, confidence and empowerment programs, and online misogynist movements as a linked cultural formation. Its central contribution is the economy of visibility, in which feminist messages succeed to the degree that they are marketable and individualized, while structural critique is muted. It also shows popular misogyny adopting the language of injury and empowerment, a mirror image of popular feminism. A limit is that the analysis centers United States and British media and largely white, middle-class forms of feminism, which Dosekun's transnational work pushes against. It connects to Fredrickson et al. on self-surveillance, Hearn on branding, chapter 14 on commodification, and the manosphere research noted in chapter 6.","themes":["popular feminism","popular misogyny","economy of visibility","postfeminism","branding"],"confidence":"uncertain","note":"Syllabus lists only Banet-Weiser. Chose Empowered (2018), likely the introduction or chapter 1; the alternative is Authentic (NYU Press, 2012). Confirm which chapter is assigned."},{"id":"dosekun-2015","course":"mfjs4160","date":"2026-11-04","status":"upcoming","kind":"article","short":"Dosekun (2015)","title":"For Western girls only? Post-feminism as transnational culture","apa":"Dosekun, S. (2015). For Western girls only? Post-feminism as transnational culture. *Feminist Media Studies, 15*(6), 960-975. https://doi.org/10.1080/14680777.2015.1062991","access":"free","read_url":"https://sro.sussex.ac.uk/id/eprint/63369/","doi_url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/14680777.2015.1062991","isbn10":null,"summary":"Dosekun challenges the assumption that postfeminism is a Western phenomenon that non-Western women only receive secondhand. Drawing on research with class-privileged women in Lagos, Nigeria, she argues that postfeminism is a transnationally circulating culture that women elsewhere can take up on their own terms.","analysis":"The article combines theoretical critique with qualitative interviews with young, affluent Nigerian women about fashion and femininity. Its contribution is a transnational analytic that refuses both the Western default in postfeminism studies and the idea that non-Western women are merely imitating. It highlights class as the axis that makes postfeminist consumption available across borders. A limit, which Dosekun acknowledges, is that her participants are an elite minority, so the account says more about global class formation than about Nigerian women broadly. It directly corrects the Western frame of Banet-Weiser and chapter 8's body image research, and it echoes Henderson's call to decenter whiteness in media studies.","themes":["postfeminism","transnational culture","Nigeria","class","femininity"],"confidence":"likely","note":"Syllabus lists only Dosekun. Chose this open-access article over Fashioning postfeminism (University of Illinois Press, 2020) because it is the article-length statement of the same argument. read_url is the Sussex repository record, open access since 2017. Some records list issue 4; Sussex and Taylor and Francis list 15(6), 960-975."},{"id":"couldry-mejias-2019","course":"mfjs4160","date":"2026-11-09","status":"upcoming","kind":"article","short":"Couldry & Mejias (2019)","title":"Data colonialism: Rethinking big data's relation to the contemporary subject","apa":"Couldry, N., & Mejias, U. A. (2019). Data colonialism: Rethinking big data's relation to the contemporary subject. *Television & New Media, 20*(4), 336-349. https://doi.org/10.1177/1527476418796632","access":"free","read_url":"https://eprints.lse.ac.uk/89511/","doi_url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/1527476418796632","isbn10":null,"summary":"Couldry and Mejias argue that the continuous extraction of data from everyday life is best understood as a new form of colonialism rather than only a new phase of capitalism. Data relations, they claim, appropriate human life as a resource in the way historical colonialism appropriated land and labor.","analysis":"This is a theoretical essay that builds its case by analogy with historical colonialism and by extending Marx's account of how capitalism annexes new domains. Its contribution is to name data extraction as appropriation, which explains why it is treated as natural and free and why it enables discrimination and behavioral manipulation. The framework revives Smythe's audience commodity and chapter 14's commodification for the platform age. Critics, including decolonial scholars, argue that the colonial analogy risks flattening the violence of historical colonialism and centers Northern platforms and theorists, though the authors explicitly include China's platforms. It connects to Roberts on the labor behind platforms, to Smythe, and to research on algorithmic classification.","themes":["data colonialism","big data","platforms","political economy","surveillance"],"confidence":"likely","note":"Syllabus lists Couldry and Mejias; chose the Television & New Media article (online 2018) over the book The costs of connection (Stanford University Press, 2019). read_url is the LSE Research Online record with the accepted manuscript."},{"id":"roberts-2019","course":"mfjs4160","date":"2026-11-09","status":"upcoming","kind":"book","short":"Roberts (2019)","title":"Behind the screen: Content moderation in the shadows of social media","apa":"Roberts, S. T. (2019). *Behind the screen: Content moderation in the shadows of social media*. Yale University Press.","access":"book","read_url":null,"doi_url":null,"isbn10":"0300235887","summary":"Roberts documents the hidden workforce of commercial content moderators who screen violent, abusive, and disturbing material for social media platforms. Based on interviews with moderators in the United States and the Philippines, she shows how this labor is outsourced, low status, psychologically harmful, and deliberately kept out of view.","analysis":"The book draws on years of interviews and fieldwork across in-house, contract, and offshore moderation settings. Its central contribution is to make commercial content moderation visible as a core function of platforms, undercutting the image of platforms as neutral conduits. It shows how global outsourcing maps moderation onto older colonial and racialized divisions of labor. A limit is that access constraints mean the evidence comes from a limited set of workers and firms, and the book predates the large-scale shift to automated and AI-assisted moderation. It pairs with Couldry and Mejias on extraction, extends Smythe's labor analysis, and complicates chapter 3's normative debates about platform responsibility.","themes":["content moderation","digital labor","platforms","outsourcing","invisible labor"],"confidence":"likely","note":"Syllabus lists only Roberts; chose Sarah T. Roberts, Behind the screen. ISBN is the 2019 Yale hardcover; confirm which chapter is assigned."},{"id":"palmer-2024","course":"mfjs4650","date":"2026-09-14","status":"read","kind":"chapter","short":"Palmer (2024)","title":"Following the historical paths of global communication","apa":"Palmer, A. (2024). Following the historical paths of global communication. In Y. R. Kamalipour (Ed.), *Global communication: A multicultural perspective* (4th ed.). Rowman & Littlefield.","access":"book","read_url":null,"doi_url":null,"isbn10":"1538186705","summary":"Palmer opens the Kamalipour textbook with a long historical survey of how messages have crossed borders, from early writing systems, trade routes, and print to the telegraph, radio, satellites, and the internet. He treats each new medium as reorganizing who can speak across distance and on whose terms.","analysis":"The chapter's central claim is that global communication has always followed the paths of trade, empire, and religion, so present day platforms inherit older routes rather than inventing new ones. Its method is synthetic narrative history, drawing on secondary sources to build a long timeline rather than testing a specific case. The contribution is orientation: it gives students a shared chronology that later readings such as Thussu and Mattelart complicate with political economy. Its main limit is a technology led storyline that tends to center Western inventions and European imperial networks, leaving non Western systems of exchange as background. Read beside Anderson, it supplies the infrastructure for print capitalism; for Eastern Europe, the same timeline can be reread through Habsburg, Ottoman, and Soviet communication networks that do not fit a simple Atlantic story.","themes":["media history","infrastructure","empire","technology"],"confidence":"likely","note":"Chapter 1 of the 4th edition (Rowman & Littlefield, March 2024), which matches the 2024 date; the same chapter title appears in the 2007 2nd edition. Page range not confirmed."},{"id":"anderson-imagined-communities-ch3","course":"mfjs4650","date":"2026-09-14","status":"read","kind":"chapter","short":"Anderson (2006), ch. 3","title":"The origins of national consciousness","apa":"Anderson, B. (2006). The origins of national consciousness. In *Imagined communities: Reflections on the origin and spread of nationalism* (Rev. ed., pp. 37-46). Verso. (Original work published 1983)","access":"book","read_url":null,"doi_url":null,"isbn10":"1844670864","summary":"Anderson argues that print capitalism made nations imaginable: publishers seeking larger markets standardized vernacular languages, and readers of the same books and newspapers came to picture themselves as a bounded community of strangers. The chapter explains why national consciousness emerged where and when it did.","analysis":"The chapter locates the birth of the nation in a material convergence of capitalism, print technology, and linguistic diversity rather than in ancient ethnic essences. Its method is comparative historical sociology, moving quickly across European cases such as Luther's Reformation pamphlets and the decline of Latin. Its lasting contribution is to make media central to political identity, which is why every later reading on mediascapes and imagined worlds, including Appadurai, builds on it. A fair critique is that its account is European in origin and diffusionist, and later scholars such as Partha Chatterjee argue that anticolonial nationalisms were not simply copies of a European model. For Eastern Europe, the chapter is directly useful: the print languages of Czech, Polish, Hungarian, and the Baltic states were built through exactly this vernacular standardization, often against imperial languages like German and Russian.","themes":["nationalism","print capitalism","imagined communities","language"],"confidence":"confirmed","note":""},{"id":"thussu-2019-ch2","course":"mfjs4650","date":"2026-09-16","status":"read","kind":"chapter","short":"Thussu (2019), ch. 2","title":"Approaches to theorizing international communication","apa":"Thussu, D. K. (2019). Approaches to theorizing international communication. In *International communication: Continuity and change* (3rd ed.). Bloomsbury Academic.","access":"book","read_url":null,"doi_url":null,"isbn10":"1780932650","summary":"Thussu surveys the main theoretical traditions used to study international communication, from modernization and development communication through dependency and cultural imperialism to globalization, cultural studies, and public sphere approaches. He sets them side by side so students can see what each explains and what it leaves out.","analysis":"The chapter argues that no single paradigm captures international communication, and that political economy and cultural approaches need each other. Its method is a structured literature review organized by school, with short summaries of figures such as Lerner, Schramm, Schiller, and Hall. The contribution is a map of the field that the rest of the semester fills in with cases. Its limit is that the survey remains weighted toward Anglo American theory even while noting calls to internationalize media studies, a problem Thussu himself takes up in the 2022 article. For an Eastern Europe case, the chapter's Cold War modernization and propaganda frameworks are the obvious starting point, though they tend to treat the region as an object of Western and Soviet broadcasting rather than as a producer of theory.","themes":["theory","political economy","cultural imperialism","modernization"],"confidence":"likely","note":"Chapter 2 of the 3rd edition per the Bloomsbury table of contents. Chapter 1 is the historical context chapter; if the course file is historical, the assigned chapter may be ch. 1 instead."},{"id":"thussu-2022-decolonizing","course":"mfjs4650","date":"2026-09-16","status":"read","kind":"article","short":"Thussu (2022)","title":"De-colonizing global news-flows: A historical perspective","apa":"Thussu, D. K. (2022). De-colonizing global news-flows: A historical perspective. *Journalism Studies, 23*(13), 1578-1592. https://doi.org/10.1080/1461670X.2022.2083007","access":"paywalled","read_url":null,"doi_url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/1461670X.2022.2083007","isbn10":null,"summary":"Thussu traces how the global news system grew out of empire, with agencies like Reuters built along imperial telegraph lines, and revisits the 1970s UNESCO fight over a New World Information and Communication Order. He argues that US digital platforms now reproduce a similar imbalance in a new form.","analysis":"The core argument is continuity: the infrastructure and agenda setting power of global news have moved from colonial agencies to Western broadcasters to Silicon Valley platforms without breaking the pattern of Northern control. Evidence is historical and institutional, drawing on agency histories, UNESCO documents, and platform market data rather than new empirical fieldwork. Its contribution is to reconnect current talk of decolonizing media with the forgotten NWICO debates, giving the Global South a longer history of resistance. One limit is that framing platforms as neocolonial can flatten differences between platform power and territorial empire, and can underplay regional powers like China and Russia that also project news abroad. For Eastern Europe it raises a sharp question: the region was a site of Soviet counterflow in the NWICO era and is now a target of both Western platforms and Russian state media.","themes":["decolonization","NWICO","news agencies","platforms"],"confidence":"confirmed","note":"Open access status not confirmed; the Taylor & Francis page blocked automated checks."},{"id":"gadzekpo-2018","course":"mfjs4650","date":"2026-09-21","status":"read","kind":"article","short":"Gadzekpo (2018)","title":"Glocalising radio during empire","apa":"Gadzekpo, A. (2018). Glocalising radio during empire. *Obsidian: Literature and Arts in the African Diaspora, 44*(2), 164-181.","access":"paywalled","read_url":null,"doi_url":null,"isbn10":null,"summary":"Gadzekpo examines colonial era radio in the Gold Coast, now Ghana, showing how a medium introduced to serve imperial administration was reshaped by local broadcasters, languages, and audiences. The essay appears in a special issue on radio and Ghanaian literature and broadcast culture.","analysis":"The argument is that colonial radio was glocalised from the start: Ghanaian announcers, translators, and writers adapted British models to vernacular languages, local music, and local storytelling. The method is archival and biographical media history, informed by the author's related work on the pioneering broadcaster B. S. Gadzekpo. Its contribution is to move agency from London to Accra and to complicate the one way cultural imperialism model that Dorfman and Mattelart represent in the same week. A limit is that stressing local adaptation can soften the coercive frame of the colonial state, which still controlled licensing, content, and wartime propaganda. The piece offers a template for Eastern Europe as well, where Soviet and later Western international broadcasters were localized by national staff in ways that exceeded their sponsors' intentions.","themes":["radio","colonialism","Ghana","glocalization"],"confidence":"likely","note":"Citation confirmed from the author's University of Ghana faculty page (Obsidian 44(2), pp. 164-181). No verified free copy found."},{"id":"dorfman-mattelart-donald-duck","course":"mfjs4650","date":"2026-09-21","status":"read","kind":"book","short":"Dorfman & Mattelart (1971/2018)","title":"How to read Donald Duck: Imperialist ideology in the Disney comic","apa":"Dorfman, A., & Mattelart, A. (2018). *How to read Donald Duck: Imperialist ideology in the Disney comic* (D. Kunzle, Trans.). OR Books. (Original work published 1971)","access":"book","read_url":null,"doi_url":null,"isbn10":"1944869832","summary":"Written in Allende's Chile, this study reads Disney comics as carriers of capitalist and imperialist values, from the treatment of money and work to the depiction of native peoples as childlike. It became the founding text of cultural imperialism critique.","analysis":"Dorfman and Mattelart argue that seemingly innocent entertainment naturalizes consumerism and a colonial view of the Global South for young readers. Their method is ideological close reading of a large sample of Disney comics, combining Marxist and semiotic analysis. The book's importance is historical as much as analytical: it was banned and burned after the 1973 coup and helped define the Latin American school of communication research. Its main weakness, pressed later by Appadurai and audience researchers, is that it infers effects from texts and gives readers almost no capacity to resist or rework meaning. Set against Gadzekpo in the same week, it shows the two poles of the course, imposition and adaptation, and it invites comparison with Soviet era readings of Western popular culture in Eastern Europe.","themes":["cultural imperialism","ideology","Disney","Latin America"],"confidence":"confirmed","note":"Listed as discussed rather than assigned; the current OR Books English edition is used for the ISBN."},{"id":"mattelart-networking-ch2-3","course":"mfjs4650","date":"2026-09-23","status":"read","kind":"chapter","short":"Mattelart (2000), ch. 2-3","title":"The culture factory; The power of propaganda","apa":"Mattelart, A. (2000). *Networking the world, 1794-2000* (L. Carey-Libbrecht & J. A. Cohen, Trans.). University of Minnesota Press.","access":"book","read_url":null,"doi_url":null,"isbn10":"081663288X","summary":"Chapter 2 follows the rise of mass culture industries such as film, news agencies, and advertising as they became international businesses. Chapter 3 traces how states, especially during the world wars and the Cold War, turned these networks into instruments of propaganda.","analysis":"Mattelart's argument is that the dream of universal communication has always been bound up with commerce and war, so networks sold as bringing humanity together also served markets and military strategy. His method is a genealogy of ideas and institutions, moving between engineers, entrepreneurs, and propagandists across two centuries. The contribution is to show that the culture industries and propaganda machines grew from the same infrastructure, which sets up the day's documentary on Pentagon influence in Hollywood. A limit is its breadth: the book compresses many national cases and still tells the story largely from French, British, and American archives. For Eastern Europe the propaganda chapter is directly relevant, since Radio Free Europe, Radio Liberty, and Soviet external broadcasting were central battlegrounds of the Cold War information war.","themes":["propaganda","culture industries","networks","Cold War"],"confidence":"likely","note":"Chose Networking the World (2000) over Mapping World Communication (1994) because its chapters 2 and 3 are titled The culture factory and The power of propaganda, which match the paired documentary on military propaganda."},{"id":"theaters-of-war-2022","course":"mfjs4650","date":"2026-09-23","status":"read","kind":"film","short":"Stahl (2022)","title":"Theaters of war: How the Pentagon and CIA took Hollywood","apa":"Stahl, R. (Director). (2022). *Theaters of war: How the Pentagon and CIA took Hollywood* [Film]. Media Education Foundation.","access":"stream","read_url":"https://films.mediaed.org/film/theaters-of-war/1f52075b-b3fb-40ca-a148-1f5007d5c1da","doi_url":null,"isbn10":null,"summary":"This 87 minute documentary uses Freedom of Information Act documents to show how the US military and CIA shape Hollywood scripts in exchange for access to equipment, bases, and expertise. It covers productions from classic war films to superhero franchises.","analysis":"Stahl's argument is that state security agencies act as uncredited script editors, removing war crimes, torture, and failures while promoting recruitment friendly narratives. The evidence is documentary: internal production notes and correspondence obtained through FOIA, paired with interviews with researchers and veterans. Its contribution is to give Mattelart's history of propaganda a contemporary, well sourced case and to show how soft power travels through global box office. A limit is its focus on US institutions, which leaves open how audiences abroad actually read these films and how other states run similar arrangements. For Eastern Europe, it invites comparison with Russian state funding of patriotic war cinema, a parallel system worth studying on its own terms.","themes":["propaganda","Hollywood","military","soft power"],"confidence":"confirmed","note":"Available to DU students on Kanopy; the link is the distributor's film page."},{"id":"tomlinson-1999-ch1","course":"mfjs4650","date":"2026-09-28","status":"read","kind":"chapter","short":"Tomlinson (1999), ch. 1","title":"Globalization and culture","apa":"Tomlinson, J. (1999). Globalization and culture. In *Globalization and culture*. Polity Press.","access":"book","read_url":null,"doi_url":null,"isbn10":"0745613381","summary":"Tomlinson defines globalization as complex connectivity, the dense web of interconnections in modern life, and argues that culture is both shaped by and constitutive of it. He separates connectivity from ideas of proximity and a single global culture, and distinguishes culture from communication technology.","analysis":"The chapter's argument is that globalization lies at the heart of modern culture and cultural practice lies at the heart of globalization, without either being the master cause. Its method is conceptual clarification, correcting slippages in Harvey, Robertson, Ohmae, Hirst and Thompson, and Waters through vivid examples like the airline passenger and the Brent Spar campaign. Its lasting contribution is the insistence that most people experience globalization by staying in place while distant forces reshape their locality, which anticipates the streaming era. Critics such as Bergesen note that it treats connectivity as newer than it is and says little about ownership and power, a gap Appadurai's politics and the Netflix article partly fill. For Eastern Europe, the stay at home thesis fits populations who experienced 1989 and EU accession largely through media and markets arriving at home.","themes":["globalization","complex connectivity","culture","deterritorialization"],"confidence":"confirmed","note":"Page range omitted: the course file is a PDF scan without printed page numbers; add Polity pages if required."},{"id":"appadurai-1990","course":"mfjs4650","date":"2026-09-28","status":"read","kind":"article","short":"Appadurai (1990)","title":"Disjuncture and difference in the global cultural economy","apa":"Appadurai, A. (1990). Disjuncture and difference in the global cultural economy. *Public Culture, 2*(2), 1-24. https://doi.org/10.1215/08992363-2-2-1","access":"paywalled","read_url":null,"doi_url":"https://doi.org/10.1215/08992363-2-2-1","isbn10":null,"summary":"Appadurai replaces center and periphery models with five overlapping flows, ethnoscapes, mediascapes, technoscapes, finanscapes, and ideoscapes, that move along disjunctive paths. He argues that the imagination is now a social practice through which people build imagined worlds from these flows.","analysis":"The essay claims that the global cultural economy is organized by disjunctures between flows of people, machines, money, images, and ideas, so homogenization and heterogenization happen at once. Its method is theoretical and illustrative, drawing on fieldwork knowledge of Bombay, Gulf migration, and the Filipino taste for American pop. It gave media studies the scapes vocabulary and helped move the field from cultural imperialism toward indigenization and hybridity. Heyman and Campbell and political economists respond that the emphasis on flows underplays capital, borders, and unequal rights to move. The essay also names Russianization as a fear of the Baltic states and Armenia, which makes it a ready anchor for an Eastern Europe case built around a nearby dominant power rather than America.","themes":["scapes","deterritorialization","imagination","homogenization"],"confidence":"confirmed","note":""},{"id":"invisible-doctrine-2024","course":"mfjs4650","date":"2026-09-28","status":"read","kind":"film","short":"Hutchison & Sabian (2024)","title":"The invisible doctrine: The secret history of neoliberalism","apa":"Hutchison, P., & Sabian, L. (Directors). (2024). *The invisible doctrine: The secret history of neoliberalism* [Film]. Media Education Foundation.","access":"stream","read_url":"https://films.mediaed.org/film/the-invisible-doctrine/fa6e6ba9-0d2d-451b-9ab4-1a1a2cf9ad14","doi_url":null,"isbn10":null,"summary":"Built around the writing of George Monbiot, this 75 minute documentary traces neoliberalism from a 1930s fringe philosophy to the unnamed common sense of politics and economics. It links deregulation and privatization to inequality, weakened democracy, and environmental damage, and ends with proposals for participatory democracy and shared commons.","analysis":"The film argues that neoliberalism's power comes partly from being invisible, spread through think tanks, business funded media, and policy networks until it seemed natural. Its evidence is a mix of archival footage, interviews, and Monbiot's narration, making it an argument essay on film rather than a neutral history. In the course it supplies the political economy behind the 1990s wave of media privatization that framed the Western Europe Netflix case. A limit is its advocacy tone and its focus on Anglo American actors, which leaves out how neoliberal reform was experienced elsewhere. Eastern Europe is a striking gap, since post 1989 shock therapy in Poland and Russia is among the clearest real world tests of the doctrine the film describes.","themes":["neoliberalism","privatization","political economy","ideology"],"confidence":"confirmed","note":"Directed by Peter Hutchison and Lucas Sabian with George Monbiot; cited under the directors per APA film format. Kanopy access through DU."},{"id":"idiz-2025-window-dressing","course":"mfjs4650","date":"2026-09-28","status":"read","kind":"article","short":"Idiz et al. (2025)","title":"Culture as window dressing? A threefold methodological framework for researching the locality of Netflix series","apa":"Idiz, D. R., Noordegraaf, J., & Vliegenthart, R. (2025). Culture as window dressing? A threefold methodological framework for researching the locality of Netflix series. *Critical Studies in Television, 20*(1), 93-117. https://doi.org/10.1177/17496020241235579","access":"free","read_url":"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/17496020241235579","doi_url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/17496020241235579","isbn10":null,"summary":"The authors combine Top 10 industry data, close reading of five crime series, and coded audience reviews to test whether Netflix's European productions deliver local culture. They find that locality appears mainly as touristic backdrop and that reviewers rarely judge series on it.","analysis":"The article argues that Netflix's European Originals are placed rather than place based: landmarks and topical themes signal locality while a transnational house style drives the storytelling. Its method is the threefold framework itself, linking catalogue data, textual analysis, and reception coding through a shared set of locality markers. The contribution is a replicable instrument for EU cultural policy debates, suggesting that content quotas count the wrong thing if culture is window dressing. Limits include a small textual sample, reliance on Netflix's own promotional data, and review sites that hide reviewer nationality, so locality is measured from the supply side. The framework transfers well to Eastern Europe, where Netflix's Polish Originals such as 1983 and The Border offer a test of whether a smaller market's locality survives the house style.","themes":["Netflix","locality","cultural policy","methods"],"confidence":"confirmed","note":"Open access (CC BY). The first author's initials are D. R. (Daphne Rena), not S."},{"id":"tomlinson-1999-ch4","course":"mfjs4650","date":"2026-09-30","status":"read","kind":"chapter","short":"Tomlinson (1999), ch. 4","title":"Deterritorialization: The cultural condition of globalization","apa":"Tomlinson, J. (1999). Deterritorialization: The cultural condition of globalization. In *Globalization and culture*. Polity Press.","access":"book","read_url":null,"doi_url":null,"isbn10":"0745613381","summary":"Tomlinson develops deterritorialization as the weakening tie between culture and place, lived through everyday media, food, shopping, and home life rather than dramatic travel. He answers objections about pre modern localism and unevenness and pairs the concept with reterritorialization and hybridity.","analysis":"The chapter argues that globalization transforms locality rather than erasing it, producing an ambivalent mix of familiarity and difference, wider horizons and new vulnerabilities. Its method is phenomenological, working through Auge's non places, Williams's patriotic couple, global food, and Garcia Canclini's Tijuana. Its contribution is a vocabulary for the mundane globalization of people who never leave home, including the idea that anchoring identity to the nation now takes active work. Massey, quoted in the chapter, supplies the strongest critique: unevenness is not only about access but about who controls flows, and Tomlinson's reply keeps the affluent Western subject near the center. For Eastern Europe, the fall of the Berlin Wall appears in the chapter as a deterritorializing event, and post 1989 labor migration to the UK is a live example of reterritorialized communities.","themes":["deterritorialization","locality","hybridity","everyday life"],"confidence":"confirmed","note":"Page range omitted: the course scan is cited by PDF page in her notes; add Polity pages if required."},{"id":"massey-1994-global-sense","course":"mfjs4650","date":"2026-09-30","status":"read","kind":"chapter","short":"Massey (1994)","title":"A global sense of place","apa":"Massey, D. (1994). A global sense of place. In *Space, place, and gender* (pp. 146-156). University of Minnesota Press.","access":"book","read_url":null,"doi_url":null,"isbn10":"0816626170","summary":"Massey asks whether a sense of place can be progressive and outward looking rather than defensive and nostalgic. She introduces the power geometry of time space compression and redefines places as meeting points of wider social relations, using her own Kilburn High Road as the example.","analysis":"The essay's central move is to reject both the idea that globalization dissolves place and the reactionary defense of bounded, authentic communities. Its method is theoretical argument grounded in close observation of a London street, combined with pointed examples like the woman collecting water and Rio's favela residents. The power geometry concept is its lasting contribution, showing that some groups initiate flows, some receive them, and some are trapped by them. A limit is that the essay is short and programmatic, offering few tools for measuring power geometry empirically, and it still starts from a Western metropolitan vantage it criticizes. For Eastern Europe, it offers a way to read post 1989 nationalist heritage politics and migrant flows as struggles over who controls mobility rather than simple losses of authenticity.","themes":["power geometry","place","mobility","feminist geography"],"confidence":"confirmed","note":"Free link is the original 1991 Marxism Today version hosted by the Amiel and Melburn Trust archive; the course text is the 1994 book reprint, which is lightly revised."},{"id":"marcus-1995-multisited","course":"mfjs4650","date":"2026-09-30","status":"read","kind":"article","short":"Marcus (1995)","title":"Ethnography in/of the world system: The emergence of multi-sited ethnography","apa":"Marcus, G. E. (1995). Ethnography in/of the world system: The emergence of multi-sited ethnography. *Annual Review of Anthropology, 24*, 95-117. https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev.an.24.100195.000523","access":"paywalled","read_url":null,"doi_url":"https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev.an.24.100195.000523","isbn10":null,"summary":"Marcus reviews an emerging style of ethnography that follows its object across multiple locations instead of staying in one village or site. He lays out six strategies for constructing such a field: follow the people, the thing, the metaphor, the plot or story, the life, and the conflict.","analysis":"The article argues that the world system cannot be treated as mere context for a local study; it has to be traced through chains of connected sites. Its method is a review essay that organizes existing studies, from Malinowski to Emily Martin, into a typology of research designs. Its contribution is practical: it turns abstract globalization theory such as Appadurai's scapes into a method a researcher can actually use. Critics worry that multi sited work trades depth for breadth and that the researcher's own mobility, often a Western privilege, shapes which sites get followed. For an Eastern Europe media case, follow the thing or follow the conflict offers a clear design, for example tracing a Russian or Ukrainian news story across state television, Telegram channels, and diaspora audiences.","themes":["multi-sited ethnography","methods","world system","anthropology"],"confidence":"confirmed","note":"Course file is an excerpt (pp. 105-110) of the full article."},{"id":"orgad-2012","course":"mfjs4650","date":"2026-10-05","status":"upcoming","kind":"book","short":"Orgad (2012)","title":"Media representation and the global imagination","apa":"Orgad, S. (2012). *Media representation and the global imagination*. Polity Press.","access":"book","read_url":null,"doi_url":null,"isbn10":"0745643809","summary":"Orgad examines how news, advertising, television, and online media build our pictures of distant others and of our own place in the world. She organizes the book around five imaginative frames: the other, the nation, possible lives, the world, and the self.","analysis":"Orgad argues that media representations are not just accurate or biased copies of reality but resources for a global imagination that shapes how people act toward distant others. Her method is critical synthesis across representation studies, using examples such as humanitarian appeals, migration coverage, and global advertising. The contribution is to join Appadurai's imagination as social practice with Hall's representation theory into a single framework for global media. A limit is that the book is mostly a conceptual map with limited original audience research, so the effects of representations on imagination are argued more than shown. It pairs naturally with the Sub Saharan Africa case and with Eastern Europe, where Western coverage long cast the region as grey, post Soviet, and in transition.","themes":["representation","global imagination","mediated otherness","humanitarianism"],"confidence":"likely","note":"Author only on the syllabus; chose Shani Orgad's 2012 Polity book as the standard global media representation text. The specific chapter assigned is unknown."},{"id":"entman-1993-framing","course":"mfjs4650","date":"2026-10-07","status":"upcoming","kind":"article","short":"Entman (1993)","title":"Framing: Toward clarification of a fractured paradigm","apa":"Entman, R. M. (1993). Framing: Toward clarification of a fractured paradigm. *Journal of Communication, 43*(4), 51-58. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1460-2466.1993.tb01304.x","access":"paywalled","read_url":null,"doi_url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1460-2466.1993.tb01304.x","isbn10":null,"summary":"Entman offers a working definition of framing as selecting some aspects of reality and making them salient to promote a particular problem definition, causal interpretation, moral evaluation, and remedy. He argues the concept can unify scattered research across communication studies.","analysis":"The essay's core claim is that framing is a shared paradigm hidden under inconsistent usage, and that clarifying it would link studies of texts, audiences, and power. Its method is conceptual, illustrated with short examples such as Cold War coverage that framed events through a US versus Soviet lens. Its four function definition became one of the most cited in the field and structures much later work on migration and conflict coverage. A limit is that it treats framing mainly as something done by journalists and elites to audiences, leaving less room for counter framing and, in a global setting, for non Western news systems. Paired with the Refugee Selfies episode, it sets up a contrast between how mainstream coverage frames migrants and how migrants frame themselves, a dynamic visible in Eastern European reporting on the 2015 Balkan route and the 2022 Ukrainian displacement.","themes":["framing","news","agenda setting","migration"],"confidence":"likely","note":"Author only on the syllabus; chose the 1993 framing essay as the standard assignment. The alternative is Entman (2003) Cascading activation, Political Communication 20(4), if the week centers on foreign policy news."},{"id":"refugee-selfies-podcast","course":"mfjs4650","date":"2026-10-07","status":"upcoming","kind":"podcast","short":"Llamas-Rodriguez & Zimanyi (2020)","title":"Refugee selfies and the media of migration","apa":"Llamas-Rodriguez, J. (Host). (2020, December 15). Refugee selfies and the media of migration (with Eszter Zimanyi) (Season 1, Episode 11) [Audio podcast episode]. In *Global media cultures*. Acast. https://shows.acast.com/global-media-cultures/episodes/refugee-selfies-and-the-media-of-migration","access":"stream","read_url":"https://shows.acast.com/global-media-cultures/episodes/refugee-selfies-and-the-media-of-migration","doi_url":null,"isbn10":null,"summary":"Eszter Zimanyi discusses refugee selfies posted to Instagram during the 2015 European migration crisis, found through the platform's location tags. She reads them as digital transience, a way for migrants to mark presence in places they are passing through.","analysis":"The episode argues that refugee selfies interrupt the dominant news frames of threat or victimhood by letting migrants author their own images of movement and place. Zimanyi's method is visual and platform analysis of public Instagram posts tied to geotagged locations along the route. The contribution is to shift attention from representation of refugees to self representation by refugees, and to show how platform features shape that possibility. A limit is that public selfies are a skewed sample of those with phones, connectivity, and safety to post, and platform visibility can also expose migrants to surveillance. Because the Balkan route runs through Hungary, Serbia, Croatia, and Slovenia, the episode maps directly onto Eastern Europe as a transit zone and onto the border politics of those states.","themes":["migration","self-representation","Instagram","refugees"],"confidence":"likely","note":"Matched on title: Global Media Cultures podcast, hosted by Juan Llamas-Rodriguez, the only podcast episode found with this title."},{"id":"thussu-2007-contraflow","course":"mfjs4650","date":"2026-10-12","status":"upcoming","kind":"chapter","short":"Thussu (2007)","title":"Mapping global media flow and contra-flow","apa":"Thussu, D. K. (2007). Mapping global media flow and contra-flow. In D. K. Thussu (Ed.), *Media on the move: Global flow and contra-flow*. Routledge.","access":"book","read_url":null,"doi_url":null,"isbn10":"0415354579","summary":"Thussu maps the dominant flow of media from the United States and Europe alongside contra-flows from places such as India, Brazil, Mexico, Qatar, and China. He distinguishes transnational contra-flows with global reach from geo-cultural ones serving regional and diaspora audiences.","analysis":"The chapter argues that US dominance persists but is no longer total, as Bollywood, telenovelas, Al Jazeera, and Asian television build audiences beyond their home markets. The method is descriptive political economy using industry revenues, audience reach, and corporate histories. Its contribution is the flow and contra-flow vocabulary, which gives the cultural imperialism debate a more plural map without dropping power. A limit is that contra-flows are often small, commercially driven, and sometimes state sponsored, so they may mirror rather than challenge the dominant model, a point Thussu concedes. For Eastern Europe the framework raises the case of Russia Today as a state funded contra-flow and of Polish and Czech streaming exports as smaller geo-cultural flows.","themes":["contra-flow","media flows","political economy","Global South"],"confidence":"likely","note":"Chose Thussu's opening chapter in his edited volume Media on the Move (Routledge, 2007 per the syllabus; some catalogs list 2006). Page range not confirmed."},{"id":"sharma-2014-meantime","course":"mfjs4650","date":"2026-10-14","status":"upcoming","kind":"book","short":"Sharma (2014)","title":"In the meantime: Temporality and cultural politics","apa":"Sharma, S. (2014). *In the meantime: Temporality and cultural politics*. Duke University Press.","access":"book","read_url":null,"doi_url":null,"isbn10":"0822355477","summary":"Sharma studies how different groups live time under global capitalism, from frequent flyers and taxi drivers to office workers and slow living advocates. She argues that speed is not experienced equally and that some people's time is maintained by others' labor.","analysis":"The book's central claim is that time, like Massey's space, has a power geometry: the jet lagged executive's temporal comfort depends on drivers, cleaners, and service workers whose time is shaped around it. Sharma's method mixes ethnography, interviews, and cultural analysis of airports, taxis, yoga at work, and slow movements. The contribution is a temporal politics of globalization that corrects celebratory accounts of speed and connectivity, including Tomlinson's culture of speed. A limit is its concentration on North American settings, which leaves open how temporal inequality looks in the Global South. If this is the assigned Sharma, it connects directly to Polson's work on privileged mobility and offers Eastern Europe a lens on migrant care and transport labor that sustains Western European time.","themes":["temporality","mobility","labor","power"],"confidence":"uncertain","note":"Author only on the syllabus and the course week is a midterm quiz day. Chose Sarah Sharma's In the Meantime because it extends Massey's power geometry and fits Polson's research on mobility; could also be a different Sharma text on globalization. Confirm against the syllabus."},{"id":"candidatu-2019-digital-diasporas","course":"mfjs4650","date":"2026-10-26","status":"upcoming","kind":"chapter","short":"Candidatu et al. (2019)","title":"Digital diasporas: Beyond the buzzword. Toward a relational understanding of mobility and connectivity","apa":"Candidatu, L., Leurs, K., & Ponzanesi, S. (2019). Digital diasporas: Beyond the buzzword. Toward a relational understanding of mobility and connectivity. In J. Retis & R. Tsagarousianou (Eds.), *The handbook of diasporas, media, and culture* (pp. 31-47). Wiley.","access":"free","read_url":"https://dspace.library.uu.nl/bitstream/handle/1874/385484/Digital_Diaspora_CandidatuLeursPonzanesi_Print.pdf?sequence=1","doi_url":null,"isbn10":"1119236703","summary":"The authors review three waves of research on diasporas online, from cyberculture to ethnography to data driven studies, and propose a relational approach grounded in feminist and postcolonial theory. They draw on Glissant's ideas of relation and creolization to move beyond fixed origins.","analysis":"The chapter argues that digital diaspora has become a buzzword that hides power, treating migrants either as isolated or as celebrated cosmopolitans. Its method is a critical literature review that reads each research paradigm for what it can and cannot see. The contribution is a relational framework that treats online and offline, continuity and change, and multiple axes of inequality as entangled. A limit is that the chapter is programmatic and offers few worked cases, so the framework's practical payoff is left to later studies. For Eastern Europe, it gives a better way to study Romanian, Polish, and Ukrainian diasporas in Western Europe, whose messaging groups and media habits cross national lines in ways a simple homeland versus host model misses.","themes":["diaspora","digital media","postcolonial theory","mobility"],"confidence":"likely","note":"Chose the 2019 handbook chapter; the free copy is the authors' accepted version in the Utrecht University repository."},{"id":"punathambekar-mohan-2019","course":"mfjs4650","date":"2026-10-28","status":"upcoming","kind":"chapter","short":"Punathambekar & Mohan (2019)","title":"Introduction: Mapping global digital cultures","apa":"Punathambekar, A., & Mohan, S. (2019). Introduction: Mapping global digital cultures. In A. Punathambekar & S. Mohan (Eds.), *Global digital cultures: Perspectives from South Asia* (pp. 1-35). University of Michigan Press. https://doi.org/10.3998/mpub.9561751","access":"free","read_url":"https://library.oapen.org/bitstream/handle/20.500.12657/23985/1006150.pdf","doi_url":"https://doi.org/10.3998/mpub.9561751","isbn10":"0472131400","summary":"The editors argue that digital cultures in South Asia should be studied as central to global transformations rather than as local variants of Silicon Valley models. They organize the field around infrastructures, platforms, and publics.","analysis":"The introduction claims that global internet studies has been provincial, generalizing from US and European cases, and that South Asia reveals different relations among states, markets, and media. Its method is a framing essay that synthesizes regional scholarship and previews the volume's chapters. The contribution is the infrastructures, platforms, and publics triad, which gives students a portable way to analyze any region's digital culture. A limit is the tension between de-Westernizing and still using categories developed largely in Western platform studies. The triad works well for the Eastern Europe case, for example Estonia's e-state infrastructure, Russia's VK and Yandex platforms, and Ukrainian wartime publics on Telegram.","themes":["digital cultures","platforms","South Asia","de-Westernizing"],"confidence":"likely","note":"Chose the open access introduction to their 2019 edited volume; the week pairs it with the China case, so a specific chapter may be assigned instead."},{"id":"elkins-2019-algorithmic-cosmopolitanism","course":"mfjs4650","date":"2026-11-02","status":"upcoming","kind":"article","short":"Elkins (2019)","title":"Algorithmic cosmopolitanism: On the global claims of digital entertainment platforms","apa":"Elkins, E. (2019). Algorithmic cosmopolitanism: On the global claims of digital entertainment platforms. *Critical Studies in Media Communication, 36*(4), 376-389.","access":"paywalled","read_url":null,"doi_url":null,"isbn10":null,"summary":"Elkins examines how Netflix and similar platforms describe their recommendation systems as bringing a borderless, cosmopolitan culture to viewers everywhere. He shows how that rhetoric sits alongside geoblocking, uneven catalogues, and corporate strategy.","analysis":"The argument is that algorithmic cosmopolitanism is a corporate discourse that presents personalized taste communities as replacing national audiences while serving market expansion. Elkins's method is critical discourse analysis of executive statements, press materials, and platform design, informed by his research on regional lockout. The contribution is to name how platforms claim a universal, post national identity while still enforcing borders through licensing and geoblocking. A limit is that the analysis centers on company rhetoric more than on how viewers in different markets actually use recommendations. It connects directly to Idiz et al. and to Eastern Europe, where catalogue gaps and later entry of Netflix left many viewers with a thinner version of the promised global library.","themes":["algorithms","cosmopolitanism","Netflix","geoblocking"],"confidence":"likely","note":"Author only on the syllabus; chose the 2019 article over his book Locked Out (NYU Press, 2019). DOI not verified, so doi_url is left null."},{"id":"khalil-zayani-2022","course":"mfjs4650","date":"2026-11-02","status":"upcoming","kind":"article","short":"Khalil & Zayani (2022)","title":"Digitality and debordered spaces in the era of streaming: A Global South perspective","apa":"Khalil, J. F., & Zayani, M. (2022). Digitality and debordered spaces in the era of streaming: A Global South perspective. *Television & New Media, 23*(2), 167-183. https://doi.org/10.1177/15274764211014584","access":"paywalled","read_url":null,"doi_url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/15274764211014584","isbn10":null,"summary":"Using Saskia Sassen's idea of third spaces, the authors study Shahid, the Arab streaming platform run by MBC Group. They argue that such services work inside national regulation while also reaching across borders, creating new sites for regional cultural politics.","analysis":"The article claims that Global South streaming platforms are neither simple local answers to Netflix nor extensions of the state, but debordered spaces that operate at several scales at once. The method is a qualitative case study of Shahid's business model, content strategy, and regulatory setting, framed by Sassen's theory of globalization. Its contribution is to move the platform debate beyond Silicon Valley and show regional players reshaping flows. A limit is dependence on a single firm tied to Saudi capital, which makes it hard to separate platform logic from state influence. For Eastern Europe it offers a template for studying regional platforms such as Poland's Player or Russia's Kinopoisk, which also balance national rules against transnational audiences.","themes":["streaming","Middle East","platforms","Global South"],"confidence":"uncertain","note":"Author pair only on the syllabus. Chose their 2022 Television & New Media article; an alternative is their open access chapter Streaming the Middle East: From legacy media to digital platforms (2023) in Digital Platforms and the Global South (Routledge). Confirm against the syllabus."},{"id":"eastern-europe-tbd","course":"mfjs4650","date":"2026-11-04","status":"upcoming","kind":"article","short":"Eastern Europe case","title":"Eastern Europe regional case study (reading TBD)","apa":null,"access":null,"read_url":null,"doi_url":null,"isbn10":null,"summary":null,"analysis":null,"themes":["Eastern Europe"],"confidence":"uncertain","note":"Stacy's own regional case; reading to be chosen."},{"id":"crawford-2021-atlas-state","course":"mfjs4650","date":"2026-11-09","status":"upcoming","kind":"chapter","short":"Crawford (2021), ch. 6","title":"State","apa":"Crawford, K. (2021). State. In *Atlas of AI: Power, politics, and the planetary costs of artificial intelligence*. Yale University Press.","access":"book","read_url":null,"doi_url":null,"isbn10":"0300264631","summary":"In the chapter on the state, Crawford follows how AI systems developed for military and intelligence purposes, such as those exposed in the Snowden files, move into policing, border control, and municipal government. She shows how state and corporate surveillance have merged.","analysis":"Crawford's argument across the book is that AI is an extractive industry of minerals, labor, and data, and this chapter shows the state as both its sponsor and its customer. Her method is investigative and archival, tracing contracts, leaked documents, and vendors like Palantir across agencies. The contribution is to treat AI as infrastructure of state power rather than as neutral technology, which fits the day's pairing with Helberg. A limit is that the chapter is anchored in the US and UK security state, with less attention to how authoritarian states build their own systems. For the Russia and Eurasia case, it provides a lens for comparing Western surveillance capitalism with Russia's SORM monitoring system and Moscow's facial recognition network.","themes":["artificial intelligence","surveillance","state power","extraction"],"confidence":"uncertain","note":"Chapter not specified on the syllabus. Chose chapter 6, State, because the class pairs Crawford with Helberg and the Russia and Eurasia case; Data or Classification are plausible alternatives."},{"id":"helberg-2021-wires-of-war","course":"mfjs4650","date":"2026-11-09","status":"upcoming","kind":"book","short":"Helberg (2021)","title":"The wires of war: Technology and the global struggle for power","apa":"Helberg, J. (2021). *The wires of war: Technology and the global struggle for power*. Avid Reader Press.","access":"book","read_url":null,"doi_url":null,"isbn10":"1982144440","summary":"Helberg, a former Google policy lead on disinformation, describes a technological cold war between democracies and the authoritarian regimes of China and Russia. He divides it into a front end fight over platforms and information and a back end fight over 5G, chips, and cables.","analysis":"The book argues that control of digital infrastructure and information space is now the main arena of great power competition and that the United States is losing ground. Its method is a policy insider's narrative built on interviews, case studies of Russian interference and Chinese technology firms, and his own time at Google. The contribution for this course is to show how a Washington security perspective frames global media as a battlefield. Its main limit is that framing, which treats US platforms as defenders of democracy and largely brackets their own power and extraction, the problem Crawford and Thussu raise. For Russia and Eurasia, and for Stacy's Eastern Europe region, it is a useful primary source on how Russian disinformation in Ukraine, the Baltics, and elections is understood in US policy circles.","themes":["geopolitics","disinformation","infrastructure","Russia"],"confidence":"confirmed","note":"ISBN is the 2022 paperback; the original hardcover appeared in 2021."}]});
